# Recorded and derived data. books.jsonl is ~45 GB per slate and the viewer
# tree is ~65 GB across ~64,000 files -- far past GitHub's limits, and all of
# it is reproducible from the recorder output. Only the small MLB Stats API
# window file and the analysis results are worth committing (see the
# project-level .gitignore for those exceptions).
polymarket_orderbook/data/live/
polymarket_orderbook/data/viewer_full/
polymarket_orderbook/data/uniform/
polymarket_orderbook/data/plots/
polymarket_orderbook/data/discovery_cache.json

# The recorder also writes to the ROOT data/live/, which this file listed only
# under polymarket_orderbook/. That gap left 133 GB across four books_*.jsonl
# visible to `git add -A`:
#   data/live/books_2026-08-28.jsonl          43.63 GB
#   data/live/books_2026-08-29.jsonl          43.01 GB
#   data/live/books_2026-08-30.jsonl          25.41 GB
#   data/live/books_2026-08-27.jsonl.jsonl    21.13 GB
# Every one is far past GitHub's 100 MB per-file limit, so a push would have
# failed after uploading gigabytes. Caught with:
#   git status --porcelain -uall
# which expands untracked directories -- plain `git status` collapses them to
# a single line and hides the size entirely.
data/live/

# Generated viewer page: rebuilt by build_viewer_multi.py, churns every run.
polymarket_orderbook/viewer_full.html

# mlbmodel: API response cache and derived tables. Everything here is
# reproducible from the free APIs with `python -m mlbmodel.collect` and
# `python -m mlbmodel.odds`; only reports/ is worth committing.
data/raw/
data/processed/
reports/predictions.parquet
reports/slate_*.csv

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/

# Logs
*.log
polymarket_orderbook/logs/
